diff --git a/SPEC.md b/SPEC.md
index ec31c8d847..d12ef704c3 100644
--- a/SPEC.md
+++ b/SPEC.md
@@ -8140,6 +8140,8 @@ Command rules:
 - `caco test run` must support `--timeout <secs>` as a one-off queued runtime override for that job without changing the project default
 - when no queued test command can be resolved, `caco test run` must fail explicitly with a routing or resolution error that tells the caller whether config or autodetection was missing
 - `caco test run` must support a `--wait` flag that blocks the CLI process until the enqueued job reaches a terminal state (`passed`, `failed`, `canceled`, or `error`) and returns the final job state, exit code, and logs in a single response; this is the required invocation mode for managed bead workers to prevent background-task stranding (bd-7dbcef)
+- queued test enqueue accepts a bounded caller `idempotency_key`, scoped by project + requester and persisted with the job before success is returned. An exact replay with the same resolved command/cwd/runtime/priority returns the original job (including after daemon restart); key reuse with payload drift is rejected. `caco test list --idempotency-key <key>` is the canonical post-timeout lookup for that caller. Queue admission/spawn runs after the durable accepted response rather than extending the enqueue request (bd-cb52ab)
+- generated MCP `caco_test_run` calls inject one durable idempotency key per tool call and reuse it across transparent retries. For `wait=true`, MCP also supplies a short `--wait-budget-secs`; if the job is still queued/running when that budget expires, the tool returns success with `data.accepted:true`, job id, idempotency key, queue progress when available, and `caco test show` polling guidance while the job continues. Direct CLI `--wait` retains its configured full wait unless the caller explicitly supplies the same accepted-receipt budget (bd-cb52ab)
 - while `caco test run --wait` is still observing a queued or running job, human-readable output should periodically surface wait progress including queued position, queued jobs ahead, queued total, and running count when available; timeout JSON should include the same progress metadata under `data.wait_progress` so agents can see queue placement before an outer tool timeout (bd-1cd621)
 - when `caco test run --wait` observes a recovered daemon-restart `error`, the JSON response must preserve the job record's `retryable`, `failure_kind`, artifact paths, recovery hint, `retry_after_secs`, and `retry_command` so release/update automation can retry infrastructure loss separately from a real validation failure; if the job ID becomes unavailable despite durable recovery, JSON output must return a retryable `queue_job_unavailable` error rather than unstructured prose
 - queued test jobs may surface `warnings` alongside terminal passed/failed state; cargo-test jobs that report zero executed tests while filtering out tests must fail validation by default and warn that the filter likely matched no tests, because Cargo test filters are substring matches rather than regular expressions. Operators may explicitly opt in to zero-test cargo invocations by setting `CACO_ALLOW_ZERO_EXECUTED_CARGO_TESTS=1` (or the legacy alias `CACO_ALLOW_ZERO_TESTS=1`) in the queued command (bd-7a5536, bd-14af0f).
@@ -8183,6 +8185,8 @@ Command rules:
 - `caco build run` must resolve its command in this order: explicit request override, configured `projects[].build.command`, deterministic autodetection
 - when no queued build command can be resolved, `caco build run` must fail explicitly with a routing or resolution error that tells the caller whether config or autodetection was missing
 - `caco build run` must support a `--wait` flag that blocks the CLI process until the enqueued job reaches a terminal state (`succeeded`, `failed`, `canceled`, or `error`) and returns the final job state, exit code, and logs in a single response; this is the required invocation mode for managed bead workers whenever a heavyweight build command is needed
+- queued build enqueue accepts the same durable, project+requester-scoped `idempotency_key` contract as queued tests: persist before acceptance, exact replay returns the original job across restart, payload drift rejects, `caco build list --idempotency-key <key>` resolves a lost response, and admission/spawn starts after the accepted HTTP receipt (bd-cb52ab)
+- generated MCP `caco_build_run` calls inject and reuse one durable key across retries and bound `wait=true` with the same accepted-receipt wait budget as tests. Budget expiry returns success with job id/key/progress/poll guidance while the build continues; direct CLI full-wait behavior is unchanged unless `--wait-budget-secs` is explicit (bd-cb52ab)
 - while `caco build run --wait` is still observing a queued or running job, human-readable output should periodically surface wait progress including queued position, queued jobs ahead, queued total, and running count when available; timeout JSON should include the same progress metadata under `data.wait_progress` so agents can see queue placement before an outer tool timeout (bd-1cd621)
 - when `caco build run --wait` observes a recovered daemon-restart `error`, the JSON response must preserve the job record's `retryable`, `failure_kind`, artifact paths, and recovery hint so release/update automation can retry infrastructure loss separately from a real build failure; if the job ID becomes unavailable despite durable recovery, JSON output must return a retryable `queue_job_unavailable` error rather than unstructured prose
 - queued build jobs must surface at least the states `queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `canceled`, and `error`
